# UX220-Lab-1
My favourite thing that I have written is the TD case study in my UX portfolio. It describes the solution I created to make TD EasyWeb even Easier for users. This was the first UX case study that I created completely outside of work and school. I worked on it independently over the summer break because I wanted to create something that reflected my own interests and experiences.

It is my favourite written piece because I got to take my experience working at TD and turn it into something creative and useful. I put a lot of thought, ideas, research, and personal experience into the case study. I had coffee chats with people working in different areas of the industry, learned from their experiences, researched how people interact with digital banking, and thought about problems I had noticed through working at TD. I noticed that users had difficulty finding features such as language preferences, GIC information, credit limit increases, and online appointment booking. Many customers who already had the TD app would still come into the branch to ask how to find or manage their credit card and GIC information, even though some of this information was available digitally. This showed me that simply having information on the app does not necessarily mean it is easy for users to find or understand. Based on this, I came up with a solution for a feature that makes important banking information more accessible to navigate. I created a concept deliverable and described my solution in writing. 

What makes this case study especially important to me is the amount of effort I put into it and is a visual representation of what I learned from my experience working at TD. It gave me something meaningful to add to my portfolio that reflects my skills and experience and to share with others. Afterward, I had a coffee chat with someone who had experience working at IBM, and I showed them my portfolio and case study. It was well received, which made me feel rewarded to have someone with industry experience recognize the work I had put into it. 
